/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane19_uf_gel_polarisation -- ULTRAFILTRATION of a protein, and the
  flux that stops answering the pump.

  THE PLANT.  1 m3/h of a 10 kg/m3 bovine serum albumin solution (BSA) is
  fed at 3 bar to 5 m2 of a 30 kDa UF membrane; the permeate leaves at 1 bar,
  so the membrane sees 2 bar.

  THE LAW (`transport gelPolarisation`, Michaels 1968; Blatt et al. 1970).
  The water passes by viscous flow, J = A_w dP, and the protein is pushed to
  the wall by that flow, where it piles up by the film law
  (c_m - c_p)/(c_b - c_p) = exp(J/k), c_p = S c_m.  A protein solution does
  not concentrate without limit: at the GEL concentration c_g the wall
  layer stops being a solution.  From there the wall is pinned at c_g and
  the flux is whatever the film can carry away:

        J = k ln( c_g (1 - S) / (c_b - S c_g) )   ~  k ln(c_g / c_b)

  -- independent of pressure.  Here A_w dP would be 360 L/(m2 h), while the
  film carries k ln(c_g/c_b) = 1e-5 ln(25) m/s = 116 L/(m2 h) at the inlet:
  the module is GEL-LIMITED from its first node, and doubling the pump
  pressure would buy nothing (check_uf_gel sweeps it).  Along the channel
  the protein concentrates, c_b rises, and the flux FALLS as ln(c_g/c_b):
  the profile shows it.

  WHAT IS DECLARED AND WHERE IT COMES FROM -- read before quoting any
  number:
    * BSA (constant/components/BSA.dat): MW and formula DERIVED from the
      UniProt sequence P02769, the derivation in the record's header.  Its
      charge is not modelled.
    * the membrane (constant/assets/UF30_hypothetical.dat): a HYPOTHETICAL
      teaching membrane, A_w 5e-5 m/(s bar) = 180 L/(m2 h bar), not a
      product;
    * c_gel 250 kg/m3, S = 0.01 and k_film 1e-5 m/s: HYPOTHETICAL teaching
      values of the order the UF literature reports for albumin, not a
      measurement (the ruling of 2026-09-30: a declared sieving coefficient,
      no MWCO model, the case saying it is a hypothesis).
  So the SHAPE is the lesson -- the plateau, the flux falling along the
  channel -- and no number here may be quoted as a property of BSA or of
  any real membrane.

  NOT MODELLED, said plainly: the osmotic pressure of the protein (the gel
  model's own assumption), fouling and its decline in time (see the batch
  diafilter's Hermia laws), the protein's charge and the pH, and the
  temperature dependence of k and c_g.  No first law is published: the BSA
  record carries no heat capacity or formation enthalpy.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        UF;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( retentate  permeate );
        operation
        {
            membrane         UF30_hypothetical;
            transport        gelPolarisation;
            area             5.0;                 // m2
            length           1.0;                 // m
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           1.0e-5;              // m/s, hypothetical teaching value
            dP_feed_total    0.0 bar;
            nNodes           100;
            transportParameters
            {
                gel
                {
                    solute    BSA;
                    c_gel     250;                // kg/m3, hypothetical teaching value
                    sieving   { BSA 0.01; }       // hypothetical teaching value
                    source    "hypothetical teaching values of the order reported for albumin UF; no measurement";
                }
            }
        }
    }
);
